      en: "El Morro de Monte Cristi is a prominent limestone cliff on the northwestern coast of the Dominican Republic, rising 245 m above the Caribbean Sea. It is part of Monte Cristi National Park. The cliff is composed of Miocene-aged limestone layers resistant to erosion. At its base are salt flats and mangroves. The cliff hosts colonies of frigatebirds and brown pelicans. The surrounding area features endemic cacti and dry forest. Annual rainfall is only about 500 mm. Historically, El Morro served as a landmark for sailors. The site is also known for its caves, including Cueva de las Aguas, which contain pre-Columbian Taino petroglyphs.",
