      en: "The Yaque del Sur is the second longest river in the Dominican Republic at 183 km. It originates in the Cordillera Central at 2,400 m elevation and flows south into the Caribbean Sea. Its watershed covers 4,972 km², draining parts of San Juan, Azua, and Barahona provinces. The river is heavily used for irrigation of rice and bananas. Average discharge is 48 m³/s. Flow is perennial but reduced during the dry season (December–April). The river is habitat for the endangered Hispaniolan river turtle (Trachemys decorata). It also supports a diverse fish fauna including freshwater shrimp.",
